From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: pcs: qca-uniphy: keep the reference clock enabled

The UNIPHY reference clock feeds external 2.5G PHYs (e.g. the RTL8221B on
the IPQ5018 QDM530 lan2 port). It is normally enabled only in the PCS
mac_config path, which runs after the boot-time PHY attach - so the PHY
has no reference clock when the MDIO bus is scanned and reads back missing
(-ENODEV), leaving the port permanently down. Mark the reference clock
CLK_IS_CRITICAL so it is enabled from registration, before the MDIO scan.

This is a workaround, not the eventual fix. CLK_IS_CRITICAL says "never gate
this clock", while the actual requirement is narrower: one consumer needs it
before it can be probed. Expressing that properly needs the PHY node to take a
clocks phandle and the MDIO/PHY layer to enable it before identification, and
as of 6.18 neither drivers/net/mdio/*.c nor phy_device.c looks at a clocks
property on a PHY node - so there is no consumer side to move this to. Keep the
flag until there is one.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
+++ b/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
@@ -221,8 +221,26 @@
 	init.parent_data = &pdata;
 	init.num_parents = 1;
 	init.ops = &qca_uniphy_refclk_ops,
-	/* always derive rate from the UNIPHY register */
-	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE,
+	/*
+	 * Always derive the rate from the UNIPHY register, and keep the
+	 * reference clock enabled from registration.
+	 *
+	 * The external 2.5G PHY fed by this clock (an RTL8221B on the IPQ5018
+	 * QDM530) needs it before the MDIO bus is scanned, which happens early
+	 * in boot; the PCS mac_config that would otherwise enable it runs only
+	 * after the boot-time PHY attach, too late, and the PHY reads back
+	 * missing.
+	 *
+	 * CLK_IS_CRITICAL overstates that: the real requirement is "this one
+	 * consumer needs the clock before it can be discovered", not "this
+	 * clock must never be gated". The native shape would be a consumer
+	 * relationship - the PHY node taking a clocks phandle and the MDIO or
+	 * PHY layer enabling it before identification - but nothing in
+	 * drivers/net/mdio/ or phy_device.c consumes a clocks property on a PHY
+	 * node, so there is no consumer to hand it to yet. Until there is,
+	 * dropping this flag means the PHY disappears from the bus.
+	 */
+	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE | CLK_IS_CRITICAL,
 
 	uniphy->ref_clk.hw.init = &init;
 	uniphy->ref_clk.uniphy = uniphy;
